import Singularity.Algebra.BlowupChart

/-!
# The blowup of a regular curve on a regular surface

Let `(R, 𝔪)` be a regular local ring and let `P` be an ideal such that `R / P` is a regular local
ring with `dim R / P + 1 = dim R`. This file proves that `P = (x)` for an element
`x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²` ([Mat89, Theorem 14.2], Stacks tag 00NQ). The case `dim R = 2` and
`dim R / P = 1` is the local form of a regular curve on a regular surface. Then it proves that
the blowup of `R` along `P` does not change `R`: the chart `R[P/x]` is `R`.

## Main results

* `spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_le_of_le_sq`: if `I ⊆ 𝔪²`, then `𝔪` and the maximal ideal of
  `R / I` need the same number of generators.
* `exists_eq_span_singleton_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient`: if `R` and `R / P` are regular and
  `dim R / P + 1 = dim R`, then `P = (x)` with `x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²`.
* `exists_eq_span_singleton_of_ringKrullDim_eq_two`: the case of a regular curve on a regular
  surface.
* `idealBlowupChart`: the chart `R[I/x]` of the blowup of `R` along an ideal `I`, at an element
  `x ∈ I`. The chart `blowupChart R x` is `idealBlowupChart (maximalIdeal R) x`
  (`blowupChart_eq_idealBlowupChart`).
* `idealBlowupChart_eq_bot_of_eq_span_singleton` and `idealBlowupChartEquivOfEqSpanSingleton`:
  if `I = (x)` with `x` a nonzerodivisor, then the map `R → R[I/x]` is an isomorphism.
* `bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient`: the blowup of a regular
  local ring along a regular subvariety of codimension 1 is the identity.

## The proof

The maximal ideal of `R / P` is the image of `𝔪`, so it has `dim R - 1` generators. If
`P ⊆ 𝔪²`, then lifts of these generators generate `𝔪` modulo `𝔪²`, and by Nakayama's lemma
they generate `𝔪`. This is not possible, because `𝔪` needs `dim R` generators. Thus there is
`x ∈ P` with `x ∉ 𝔪²`.

The ring `R / (x)` is regular of dimension `dim R - 1` (`quotient_span_singleton`), thus it is a
domain (`isDomain_of_isRegularLocalRing`). Assume that `y ∈ P` and `y ∉ (x)`. The image of `y`
in `R / (x)` is not zero, thus it is a nonzerodivisor, and
`dim R / (x, y) + 1 ≤ dim R / (x) = dim R - 1`. But `R / P` is a quotient of `R / (x, y)`, so
`dim R / P + 1 ≤ dim R - 1`. This is false, because `dim R / P = dim R - 1`. Thus `P = (x)`.

## The blowup

The blowup of `Spec R` along `V(I)` is `Proj` of the Rees algebra `⊕ Iⁿ`. If `I` is generated
by `x₁, ..., x_r`, then it is covered by the charts `Spec R[I/xᵢ]`. Here `R[I/x]` is the
`R`-subalgebra of `R_x` generated by the fractions `a/x` with `a ∈ I`. This file does not
formalize `Proj`. It gives the chart `R[I/x]` and proves the statement on the chart: if
`I = (x)`, then one chart covers the blowup, and if `x` is a nonzerodivisor, then the chart is
`R`. So the blowup of a regular local ring along a regular curve on a regular surface is an
isomorphism.

## References

* [Mat89] H. Matsumura, *Commutative ring theory*, 1989.
* [Sta] The Stacks project authors, *The Stacks project*.
-/

open IsLocalRing Ideal

universe u

namespace Singularity.Algebra

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R]

/-! ### 1. A regular quotient of codimension 1 -/

/-- Let `R` be a Noetherian local ring and let `I ⊆ 𝔪²`. Then `𝔪` needs at most as many
generators as the maximal ideal of `R / I`. The lifts of generators of the maximal ideal of
`R / I` generate `𝔪` modulo `𝔪²`, and Nakayama's lemma gives the rest. -/
theorem spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_le_of_le_sq [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] {I : Ideal R}
    [IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)] (hI : I ≤ maximalIdeal R ^ 2) :
    (maximalIdeal R).spanFinrank ≤ (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).spanFinrank := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨t, htcard, htspan⟩ :=
    Submodule.FG.exists_span_finset_card_eq_spanFinrank
      (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).fg_of_isNoetherianRing
  have htspan : span (t : Set (R ⧸ I)) = maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I) := htspan
  set mk := Ideal.Quotient.mk I with hmk
  set t' : Set R := Function.surjInv Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective '' (t : Set (R ⧸ I))
    with ht'
  have himg : mk '' t' = t := by
    rw [ht', ← Set.image_comp, Function.comp_surjInv, Set.image_id]
  have ht'fin : t'.Finite := t.finite_toSet.image _
  have ht'm : t' ⊆ maximalIdeal R := by
    rintro _ ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩
    have hmkq : mk (Function.surjInv Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective q) = q :=
      Function.surjInv_eq _ _
    by_contra hnot
    have hu : IsUnit (Function.surjInv Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective q) :=
      IsLocalRing.notMem_maximalIdeal.mp hnot
    have hqm : q ∈ maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I) := htspan ▸ Ideal.subset_span hq
    exact IsLocalRing.notMem_maximalIdeal.mpr (hmkq ▸ hu.map mk) hqm
  have hle : maximalIdeal R ≤ span t' ⊔ maximalIdeal R • maximalIdeal R := by
    intro y hy
    have hy' : mk y ∈ span (mk '' t') := by
      rw [himg, htspan, maximalIdeal_quotient_eq_map]
      exact Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ hy
    rw [← Ideal.map_span, Ideal.mem_map_iff_of_surjective _ Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective] at hy'
    obtain ⟨y', hy'mem, hyy'⟩ := hy'
    have hsub : y - y' ∈ I := Ideal.Quotient.eq.mp hyy'.symm
    have hsub' : y - y' ∈ maximalIdeal R • maximalIdeal R := by
      rw [Ideal.smul_eq_mul, ← pow_two]
      exact hI hsub
    rw [show y = y' + (y - y') by ring]
    exact Submodule.add_mem_sup hy'mem hsub'
  have hm : maximalIdeal R ≤ span t' :=
    Submodule.le_of_le_smul_of_le_jacobson_bot (maximalIdeal R).fg_of_isNoetherianRing
      (maximalIdeal_le_jacobson _) hle
  have heq : maximalIdeal R = span t' := le_antisymm hm (Ideal.span_le.mpr ht'm)
  rw [heq]
  calc (span t').spanFinrank
      ≤ t'.ncard := Submodule.spanFinrank_span_le_ncard_of_finite ht'fin
    _ ≤ (t : Set (R ⧸ I)).ncard := Set.ncard_image_le t.finite_toSet
    _ = t.card := Set.ncard_coe_finset t
    _ = (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).spanFinrank := htcard

/-- Let `R` be a regular local ring and let `P` be an ideal such that `R / P` is a regular local
ring and `dim R / P + 1 = dim R`. Then `P = (x)` for an element `x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²`
([Mat89, Theorem 14.2], Stacks tag 00NQ). The ideal `P` is prime, because `R / P` is a domain. -/
theorem exists_eq_span_singleton_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    (P : Ideal R) [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)]
    (h : ringKrullDim (R ⧸ P) + 1 = ringKrullDim R) :
    ∃ x ∈ maximalIdeal R, x ∉ maximalIdeal R ^ 2 ∧ P = span {x} := by
  have hPm : P ≤ maximalIdeal R :=
    IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal (Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mp inferInstance)
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := exists_ringKrullDim_eq_natCast (R ⧸ P)
  have hR : ringKrullDim R = ((n + 1 : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
    rw [← h, hn]
    norm_cast
  have hsfR : (maximalIdeal R).spanFinrank = n + 1 := by
    have := IsRegularLocalRing.spanFinrank_maximalIdeal (R := R)
    rw [hR] at this
    exact_mod_cast this
  have hsfP : (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ P)).spanFinrank = n := by
    have := IsRegularLocalRing.spanFinrank_maximalIdeal (R := R ⧸ P)
    rw [hn] at this
    exact_mod_cast this
  -- Step 1: `P` is not in `𝔪²`.
  have hnot : ¬ (P : Set R) ⊆ (maximalIdeal R ^ 2 : Ideal R) := fun hle => by
    have := spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_le_of_le_sq (I := P) hle
    omega
  obtain ⟨x, hxP, hx2⟩ := Set.not_subset.mp hnot
  have hxm : x ∈ maximalIdeal R := hPm hxP
  -- Step 2: `R / (x)` is a regular domain of dimension `n`.
  obtain ⟨hreg, hdim⟩ := quotient_span_singleton hxm hx2
  have := hreg
  have hdom : IsDomain (R ⧸ span {x}) := isDomain_of_isRegularLocalRing _
  have hxle : span {x} ≤ P := (span_singleton_le_iff_mem _).mpr hxP
  refine ⟨x, hxm, hx2, le_antisymm ?_ hxle⟩
  -- Step 3: an element `y ∈ P \ (x)` lowers the dimension too much.
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨y, hyP, hyx⟩ := Set.not_subset.mp hne
  set y' := Ideal.Quotient.mk (span {x}) y with hy'
  have hy0 : y' ≠ 0 := fun h0 => hyx (Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.mp h0)
  have h1 := ringKrullDim_quotient_succ_le_of_nonZeroDivisor (mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_ne_zero hy0)
  let f : R ⧸ span {x} →+* R ⧸ P := Ideal.Quotient.factor hxle
  have hf : ∀ a ∈ span {y'}, f a = 0 := by
    intro a ha
    obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := mem_span_singleton'.mp ha
    rw [map_mul, hy']
    simp only [f, Ideal.Quotient.factor_mk, Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.mpr hyP, mul_zero]
  have hg : Function.Surjective (Ideal.Quotient.lift _ f hf) :=
    Ideal.Quotient.lift_surjective_of_surjective _ hf (Ideal.Quotient.factor_surjective hxle)
  have h2 := ringKrullDim_le_of_surjective _ hg
  have h3 : ringKrullDim (R ⧸ P) + 1 ≤ ringKrullDim (R ⧸ span {x}) :=
    (add_le_add h2 le_rfl).trans h1
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := exists_ringKrullDim_eq_natCast (R ⧸ span {x})
  rw [hm, hR] at hdim
  rw [hm, hn] at h3
  have e1 : m + 1 = n + 1 := by exact_mod_cast hdim
  have e2 : n + 1 ≤ m := by exact_mod_cast h3
  omega

/-- A regular curve on a regular surface is given by one equation. Let `R` be a regular local ring
of dimension 2 and let `P` be an ideal such that `R / P` is a regular local ring of dimension 1.
Then `P = (x)` for an element `x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²` ([Mat89, Theorem 14.2]). -/
theorem exists_eq_span_singleton_of_ringKrullDim_eq_two [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    (hR : ringKrullDim R = 2) (P : Ideal R) [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)]
    (hP : ringKrullDim (R ⧸ P) = 1) :
    ∃ x ∈ maximalIdeal R, x ∉ maximalIdeal R ^ 2 ∧ P = span {x} :=
  exists_eq_span_singleton_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient P (by rw [hR, hP]; rfl)

/-! ### 2. The chart of the blowup along an ideal -/

variable (R) in
/-- The chart `R[I/x]` of the blowup of `R` along an ideal `I`: the `R`-subalgebra of `R_x`
generated by the fractions `a/x` with `a ∈ I`. If `I` is generated by `x₁, ..., x_r`, then the
blowup `Proj (⊕ Iⁿ)` is covered by the spectra of the charts `R[I/xᵢ]`. -/
noncomputable def idealBlowupChart (I : Ideal R) (x : R) : Subalgebra R (Localization.Away x) :=
  Algebra.adjoin R ((fun a => algebraMap R (Localization.Away x) a *
    IsLocalization.Away.invSelf x) '' (I : Set R))

/-- The chart `R[𝔪/x]` of the blowup at the closed point is the chart of the blowup along `𝔪`. -/
theorem blowupChart_eq_idealBlowupChart [IsLocalRing R] (x : R) :
    blowupChart R x = idealBlowupChart R (maximalIdeal R) x :=
  rfl

/-- If `I = (x)`, then the chart `R[I/x]` is the image of `R` in `R_x`. -/
theorem idealBlowupChart_eq_bot_of_eq_span_singleton {I : Ideal R} {x : R} (hI : I = span {x}) :
    idealBlowupChart R I x = ⊥ := by
  subst hI
  rw [eq_bot_iff, idealBlowupChart, Algebra.adjoin_le_iff]
  rintro _ ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := mem_span_singleton'.mp ha
  show _ ∈ (⊥ : Subalgebra R (Localization.Away x))
  rw [Algebra.mem_bot]
  refine ⟨c, ?_⟩
  show algebraMap R (Localization.Away x) c =
    algebraMap R (Localization.Away x) (c * x) * IsLocalization.Away.invSelf x
  rw [map_mul, mul_assoc, IsLocalization.Away.mul_invSelf, mul_one]

/-- If `I = (x)` and `x` is a nonzerodivisor, then the map `R → R[I/x]` is bijective. -/
theorem bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_eq_span_singleton {I : Ideal R} {x : R}
    (hI : I = span {x}) (hx : x ∈ nonZeroDivisors R) :
    Function.Bijective (algebraMap R (idealBlowupChart R I x)) := by
  refine ⟨fun a b hab => ?_, fun z => ?_⟩
  · have hinj : Function.Injective (algebraMap R (Localization.Away x)) :=
      IsLocalization.injective _ (Submonoid.powers_le.mpr hx)
    exact hinj (congrArg Subtype.val hab)
  · have hz : (z : Localization.Away x) ∈ (⊥ : Subalgebra R (Localization.Away x)) := by
      rw [← idealBlowupChart_eq_bot_of_eq_span_singleton hI]
      exact z.2
    obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := Algebra.mem_bot.mp hz
    exact ⟨r, Subtype.ext hr⟩

/-- If `I = (x)` and `x` is a nonzerodivisor, then `R ≃ R[I/x]` as `R`-algebras. So the blowup of
`R` along a principal ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor is an isomorphism. -/
noncomputable def idealBlowupChartEquivOfEqSpanSingleton {I : Ideal R} {x : R}
    (hI : I = span {x}) (hx : x ∈ nonZeroDivisors R) : R ≃ₐ[R] idealBlowupChart R I x :=
  AlgEquiv.ofBijective (Algebra.ofId R _)
    (bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_eq_span_singleton hI hx)

/-! ### 3. The blowup of a regular curve on a regular surface -/

/-- The blowup of a regular local ring along a regular subvariety of codimension 1 is an
isomorphism. Let `R` be a regular local ring and let `P` be an ideal such that `R / P` is a
regular local ring and `dim R / P + 1 = dim R`. Then `P = (x)` with `x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²`, `x` is a
nonzerodivisor, and the map `R → R[P/x]` to the chart of the blowup along `P` is bijective.
Because `P = (x)`, this chart covers the blowup. The case `dim R = 2` is the blowup of a regular
curve on a regular surface ([Mat89, Theorem 14.2], Stacks tag 00NQ). -/
theorem bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient
    [IsRegularLocalRing R] (P : Ideal R) [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)]
    (h : ringKrullDim (R ⧸ P) + 1 = ringKrullDim R) :
    ∃ x ∈ maximalIdeal R, x ∉ maximalIdeal R ^ 2 ∧ P = span {x} ∧ x ∈ nonZeroDivisors R ∧
      Function.Bijective (algebraMap R (idealBlowupChart R P x)) := by
  obtain ⟨x, hxm, hx2, hP⟩ := exists_eq_span_singleton_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient P h
  have := isDomain_of_isRegularLocalRing R
  have hx0 : x ≠ 0 := by
    rintro rfl
    exact hx2 (Ideal.zero_mem _)
  have hx : x ∈ nonZeroDivisors R := mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_ne_zero hx0
  exact ⟨x, hxm, hx2, hP, hx,
    bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_eq_span_singleton hP hx⟩

/-- The blowup of a regular local ring of dimension 2 along a regular curve is an isomorphism.
Let `R` be a regular local ring of dimension 2 and let `P` be an ideal such that `R / P` is a
regular local ring of dimension 1. Then `P = (x)` with `x ∈ 𝔪 \ 𝔪²`, and the map `R → R[P/x]` to
the chart of the blowup along `P` is bijective. This chart covers the blowup. -/
theorem bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_ringKrullDim_eq_two [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    (hR : ringKrullDim R = 2) (P : Ideal R) [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)]
    (hP : ringKrullDim (R ⧸ P) = 1) :
    ∃ x ∈ maximalIdeal R, x ∉ maximalIdeal R ^ 2 ∧ P = span {x} ∧ x ∈ nonZeroDivisors R ∧
      Function.Bijective (algebraMap R (idealBlowupChart R P x)) :=
  bijective_algebraMap_idealBlowupChart_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient P
    (by rw [hR, hP]; rfl)

end Singularity.Algebra
